# 2026-09-18 (session 16): first fire of the block-holdout workflow.
#
# WHY: every quality number committed before today was ONE global DTI with no error bar, so
# "candidate A 0.0878 vs candidate B 0.0999" carried no way to ask whether 0.012 is larger than the
# noise between 51 km regions of the survey.  scripts/block_holdout_eval.py now scores the shipped
# submission per 51.2 km block (the partition src/blocks.py already uses) and bootstraps over
# blocks.  Measured in the development sandbox on the committed bytes:
#
#   reference (floor 0.1, width 0) proxy DTI 0.0999, block-bootstrap CI95 [0.0883, 0.1119]
#   best genuinely different alternative (width 1 px) 0.0878, P(beats reference) = 0.008
#   50 candidates swept -> only 10 DISTINCT emissions (the shipped raster is a hard band, so the
#   floor axis is degenerate - reported, not hidden)
#   the sandbox reproduces the runner's committed sweep row digit for digit
#   (dti 0.099859, TP_w 8378.12, FP_w 164461.741, FN_w 53285.88, 172,974 px)
#
# WHAT THIS FIRE DOES: re-runs the same measurement on a GitHub-hosted runner (independent
# numpy/rasterio build, independent host) and compares it with the report the sandbox committed -
# the `measure` job fails if the reference DTI, its three metric components, the support size or the
# bootstrap interval move.  TRAIN_FOLDS is set to ONE fold (0) so the generalisation reading is
# measured without spending four 300-minute jobs on a first look: fold 0 trains with whole 51 km
# blocks held out (configs/config_block_holdout.yaml) and is then scored twice - on the blocks it
# never saw and on the blocks it trained on - which is the memorisation-vs-transfer gap the
# scattered-window holdout cannot produce.
#
# Parameters: .github/triggers/block-holdout-params
